        <mxCell id="dm15" value="4.1.4: send(advisor.email, &quot;Advisee dropped a critical course&quot;)" style="html=1;verticalAlign=bottom;endArrow=block;endFill=1;rounded=0;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"><mxPoint x="1350" y="872" as="sourcePoint" /><mxPoint x="1530" y="872" as="targetPoint" /></mxGeometry>
        </mxCell>

        <mxCell id="dNote1" value="&lt;b&gt;This diagram is the single clearest piece of evidence for the Observer pattern ④.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Read the left half and the right half separately:&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Left of the red line&lt;/b&gt; — the core enrolment logic. It performs a transaction, publishes one event, and returns.&lt;br&gt;It contains &lt;u&gt;no reference&lt;/u&gt; to WaitlistObserver, AdvisorObserver, or EmailChannel, and never learns whether&lt;br&gt;a notification was delivered.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Right of the red line&lt;/b&gt; — the notification fan-out. Adding a fourth recipient (department head, the future&lt;br&gt;financial aid system) means writing one new ConcreteObserver and calling attach(). &lt;b&gt;Zero lines of&lt;br&gt;enrolment code change&lt;/b&gt; — the Open/Closed Principle in practice.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;This is what the brief means by &lt;i&gt;&quot;automated and decoupled from the core enrolment logic.&quot;&lt;/i&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#fff2cc;strokeColor=#d6b656;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=10;spacingTop=8;fontSize=11;dashed=1;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="35" y="620" width="900" height="230" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="dNote2" value="&lt;b&gt;Why a single instance — and why &lt;u&gt;not&lt;/u&gt; the Singleton pattern.&lt;/b&gt; Exactly one observer registry must exist: if a second one were created, subscriptions registered at start-up would be invisible to the instance dispatching events, and notifications would vanish silently. We get that guarantee from the &lt;b&gt;composition root&lt;/b&gt; — composition_root.py constructs one NotificationPublisher and injects the same reference everywhere — rather than from Singleton, which would have conflicted with SRP, DIP and OCP for no additional benefit. See the rejected-patterns panel on diagram 03." style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#f8cecc;strokeColor=#b85450;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=10;spacingTop=8;fontSize=11;dashed=1;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="35" y="870" width="900" height="80" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>

      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
</mxfile>
